// heartsim tutorial: AV blocks, sinus pauses and bradycardias. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "05_av_blocks",
    title: "AV blocks and bradycardias",
    summary: "The PR interval as the AV node's delay, then first-degree, Wenckebach, Mobitz II and complete block, escape rhythms, sinus pauses, and how a dual-chamber pacemaker restores AV synchrony.",
    references: [
        "Kusumoto FM et al. 2018 ACC/AHA/HRS guideline on the evaluation and management of patients with bradycardia and cardiac conduction delay. Circulation 2019;140:e382-e482.",
        "Goldberger AL, Goldberger ZD, Shvilkin A. Goldberger's Clinical Electrocardiography: A Simplified Approach, 9th ed. Elsevier 2017 (ch. 17).",
        "Glikson M et al. 2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiac pacing and cardiac resynchronization therapy. Eur Heart J 2021;42:3427-3520.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: first-degree, Mobitz I, Mobitz II and third-degree AV block, junctional and ventricular escape rhythms, sinus pause. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "The PR interval is the AV node's delay",
            markdown: r#"
The AV node is a **gatekeeper**. It delays every impulse by about a tenth of a second so that the
ventricles can fill before they contract. On the ECG that delay is the **PR interval**, measured from
the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex.

* Normal PR: **0.12 to 0.20 s** (120 to 200 ms).
* A block is a failure of the gate: the impulse is delayed (1st degree), only sometimes stopped
  (2nd degree), or never passes (3rd degree).

Normal sinus rhythm is loaded. Find the PR interval in lead II: it is well inside the normal range.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A PR interval of 0.16 s is:",
                choices: ["Normal", "First-degree AV block", "A short PR, suggesting pre-excitation", "Complete heart block"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "0.16 s is 160 ms, inside the normal range of 120 to 200 ms. Over 200 ms is first-degree block; under 120 ms suggests pre-excitation or a junctional rhythm.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "First degree: a longer PR",
            markdown: r#"
In **first-degree AV block** every P wave is followed by a QRS, but the **PR interval is longer than
0.20 s** and stays the same from beat to beat. Nothing is dropped; the gatekeeper is just slow.

It is often harmless. Causes: high vagal tone, athletes, beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers,
digoxin, inferior myocardial infarction, and ageing of the conduction system.

This strip has a PR of about 230 ms. Measure it against the 5 small boxes (0.20 s) of the grid.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_1st")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In first-degree AV block, how many P waves fail to conduct?",
                choices: ["None, the PR is just prolonged", "Every second P wave", "Every P wave", "Only after a premature beat"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "First-degree block is a delay, not a failure: every P is followed by a QRS, with a constant PR interval above 0.20 s.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Second degree, Mobitz I (Wenckebach)",
            markdown: r#"
In **Mobitz type I** the AV node tires: the **PR interval lengthens beat after beat** until one P
wave is not conducted and a QRS is dropped. The node rests during the pause and the cycle starts
again with a short PR.

Two clues on the strip:

* "longer, longer, longer, drop",
* the R–R intervals get *shorter* before the pause (the PR increases by smaller steps), which gives
  **grouped beating**.

The block is almost always **in the AV node**, with a narrow QRS. It is often benign, seen in
athletes and during sleep, and needs a pacemaker only if it causes symptoms.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_2nd_mobitz1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What is the key finding of Mobitz type I block?",
                choices: [
                    "A PR interval that progressively lengthens until a P wave is dropped",
                    "A constant PR interval with a sudden dropped QRS",
                    "P waves with no relationship to the QRS",
                    "A PR interval over 0.20 s in every beat",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Progressive PR lengthening before a dropped beat is the Wenckebach pattern. A constant PR with a sudden dropped beat is Mobitz II, and no relationship at all is complete block.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Second degree, Mobitz II",
            markdown: r#"
In **Mobitz type II** the PR interval is **constant** in all the conducted beats, and then, without
warning, a P wave is not followed by a QRS.

The block is usually **below the AV node**, in the His bundle or the bundle branches, so the QRS is
often **wide**. It cannot be predicted and it can suddenly become complete heart block, so Mobitz II
usually needs a **permanent pacemaker**, even when the patient feels well.

In the simulator three of every four P waves are conducted with a bundle-branch block pattern. The
PR does not change before the pause.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_2nd_mobitz2_4to3")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Why is Mobitz II more worrying than Mobitz I?",
                choices: [
                    "The block is below the AV node and can progress suddenly to complete heart block",
                    "It always makes the heart rate faster",
                    "It is always caused by drugs",
                    "It only occurs in athletes",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Infranodal block does not warn with a lengthening PR and has no reliable escape rhythm if it becomes complete. Mobitz I is nodal, usually stable and benign.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Complete block with a junctional escape",
            markdown: r#"
In **third-degree (complete) AV block** no impulse crosses the node. The atria keep beating at the
sinus rate; the ventricles are driven by an **escape pacemaker**. The two rhythms are **independent**:
this is **AV dissociation**, and P waves march through the QRS complexes with a changing PR interval.

When the block is in the AV node, the escape comes from the **junction**: a narrow QRS at **40 to
60 beats per minute**. Here the P waves run at 80 and the QRS complexes at 45.

Pick one P wave and one QRS: their distance is different every time.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_3rd_junctional")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "P waves at 80 bpm, narrow QRS complexes at 45 bpm, and a different PR interval on every beat. What is this?",
                choices: [
                    "Complete AV block with a junctional escape rhythm",
                    "First-degree AV block",
                    "Mobitz I block",
                    "Sinus bradycardia",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Atria and ventricles beat independently at their own rates, so the PR interval changes continuously. The narrow QRS at 45 bpm is a junctional escape.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Complete block with a ventricular escape",
            markdown: r#"
When the block is **below the AV junction**, the junction is cut off too and the escape pacemaker
lies in the ventricular muscle or Purkinje fibres. It is **slower (20 to 40 beats per minute)**,
less reliable, and the **QRS is wide**.

This is an emergency. The patient may faint or go into cardiac arrest. Atropine works on the AV node,
so it often fails when the block is infranodal. The rescue is **pacing**, first with a temporary
wire or external pads, then a permanent device.

Compare with the previous page: the same P waves, but now the QRS is wide and the rate lower.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_3rd_ventricular")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What does a wide-complex escape rhythm at 35 bpm tell you about the site of the block?",
                choices: [
                    "It is below the AV node, and the escape is unreliable",
                    "It is in the SA node",
                    "It is in the atria",
                    "It is a normal variant",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A wide, slow escape arises in the ventricles, which means the junction is not available as a backup. This is a high-risk block that needs pacing.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A pause with no P wave",
            markdown: r#"
A problem in the sinus node itself gives **pauses without any P wave**. In **sinus arrest** the node
simply stops firing for a while and then restarts at an unrelated time, so the pause is **not a
multiple of the normal P–P interval**.

If the pause is long enough, a lower pacemaker takes over. Here the AV junction produces narrow
**escape beats** at 45 bpm during a 3-second pause, with no P wave in front of them.

Pauses of 3 seconds or more, or any pause with symptoms, deserve investigation.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_arrest_junctional_escape")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "During a sinus pause a narrow QRS appears with no P wave before it. What is it?",
                choices: [
                    "A junctional escape beat",
                    "A premature atrial beat",
                    "A ventricular premature beat",
                    "A paced beat",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "When the sinus node fails, the AV junction becomes the pacemaker after a delay. Its beats are narrow, late rather than early, and have no preceding P wave.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A pause that fits the P-P interval",
            markdown: r#"
In **sinoatrial exit block** the node keeps firing regularly, but some impulses never leave it. The
inside clock is not disturbed, so the pause is **exactly a multiple of the basic P–P interval**: two,
three, or more.

That is the difference from sinus arrest. Measure the P–P interval around the pause and compare it
with the normal P–P interval: here every third impulse fails and the pause is exactly twice the
normal P–P.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sa_exit_block")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "How does the pause of SA exit block differ from that of sinus arrest?",
                choices: [
                    "It is an exact multiple of the normal P-P interval",
                    "It is always longer than 3 seconds",
                    "It always has an escape beat",
                    "It has a wide QRS",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "In exit block the sinus clock keeps its rhythm and the missing P wave falls exactly where it was due. In arrest the clock stops and restarts, so the pause has no fixed relationship to the P-P interval.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A pacemaker restores AV synchrony",
            markdown: r#"
A **dual-chamber (DDD) pacemaker** has a wire in the right atrium that **senses** each P wave and a
wire in the right ventricle that **paces** it after a programmed **AV delay**, here 150 ms. The
heart again follows the sinus rate, and atria and ventricles contract in order.

Look for the thin vertical **pacing spike** before every wide QRS, and notice that the QRS rate
equals the P-wave rate. Complete heart block is still there underneath: the device is doing the
conduction.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("paced_ddd")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(Highlight("pacemaker")),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "In DDD pacing with complete heart block, what triggers each ventricular spike?",
                choices: [
                    "The sensed P wave, followed by the AV delay",
                    "A fixed timer that ignores the atria",
                    "The previous QRS complex",
                    "The T wave",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A DDD pacemaker tracks the atrium. It senses the P wave and delivers a ventricular pulse after the programmed AV delay, so the ventricles keep pace with the sinus node.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
